  "helpSearchHint": "Search help topics",
  "helpEmptySearchResult": "No results found",
  "helpStartSectionTitle": "Getting Started",
  "helpStartOnboardingTitle": "Setting up ShiftBell",
  "helpStartOnboardingBody": "Allow notifications, exact alarms and display over other apps so ShiftBell can alert you reliably. Then choose whether your shifts repeat, add your shift names and set alarms for the shifts that need them. You can skip alarms and add them later in Settings.\n\nRemove unused shift names with × and add any missing names with + Add. If you have used ShiftBell before, choose Restore from backup to restore a backup file.",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesTitle": "Repeating vs. irregular schedules",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesBody": "A repeating schedule follows a cycle, such as Day, Day, Night, Night, Off, Off. Build the cycle and select today’s position in it. ShiftBell fills in future dates automatically.\n\nFor a non-repeating schedule, assign shifts to dates yourself. Long-press a date, select any other dates you want, and choose a shift.",
  "helpShiftSectionTitle": "Managing Shifts",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignTitle": "Adding shifts without a repeating pattern",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignBody": "Touch and hold a date on the calendar, select any other dates you need, then choose a shift. The shift is assigned only to the dates you select.\n\nAdd upcoming shifts in advance so ShiftBell can schedule their alarms.",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftTitle": "Changing the shift on a specific date",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftBody": "Whether you're on a repeating or irregular schedule, you can override a single date by long-pressing it on the calendar and picking a different shift. Alarms for that day are automatically recalculated to match the new shift.",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsTitle": "Customizing shift colors",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsBody": "Every calendar theme has default colors per shift, but you can override the color of a specific shift name if you like. Pick the shift name in the shift settings and choose a color — your choice takes priority over the theme's default.",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsTitle": "Viewing other teams in your rotation",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsBody": "Tap All teams on the Calendar tab to compare team schedules. Add team names and choose your team. You can use one repeating pattern with a different starting position for each team, or set a separate cycle or weekly schedule for each team.\n\nTeam schedules are calculated on your device. They do not connect to coworkers’ accounts or download their schedules.",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeTitle": "When your team changes",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeBody": "If you have set up team schedules, open All teams → Edit → Change my team and select your new team. Shift alarms will follow that team’s schedule. Manual changes to individual dates will be cleared, but the team schedules will be kept.\n\nTo change your position in a repeating pattern instead, go to Settings → Edit → Change Schedule. Check the confirmation message before saving.",
  "helpAlarmSectionTitle": "Alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesTitle": "Setting alarms for a shift",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesBody": "Add up to five alarms per shift in Settings. Each alarm has its own time and sound settings and can ring on the shift day, the day before or the day after.\n\nShiftBell prepares upcoming alarms automatically as your calendar moves forward. For a non-repeating schedule, assign your shifts ahead of time so their alarms can be scheduled.\n\nAlarm lists use a 24-hour display. The time editor follows your device’s 12- or 24-hour setting. In 12-hour mode, 12 AM is midnight and 12 PM is noon.\n\nShift alarms follow the device’s current time zone. When daylight saving time skips a clock time, the alarm moves forward by the clock change (for example, 02:30 becomes 03:30 for a one-hour jump). When a clock time occurs twice, it rings once at the second occurrence. Alarms that resolve to the same time are combined.",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeTitle": "Choosing an alarm sound and volume",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeBody": "You can choose from 7 built-in alarm sounds or your device's default alarm sound. Speaker characteristics vary by device, so the app auto-corrects volume, but if it still feels off, try switching to a different sound in the alarm settings.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingTitle": "If an alarm doesn't go off",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingBody": "Check that exact alarms are allowed for ShiftBell. Also check notification and display-over-other-apps permissions so you can see and control alarms, and review your phone’s battery restrictions.\n\nMake sure the date has a shift assigned and that the shift has an alarm. Check the alarm’s sound, volume and vibration settings as well.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryTitle": "Viewing alarm history",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryBody": "You can see every alarm and its result under Settings → Alarm History.\n\nSnooze sets the alarm for five minutes after you tap it, even when the clocks change. During the repeated hour when clocks go back, “First occurrence” and “Second occurrence” distinguish the two times in upcoming alarms and alarm history.",
  "helpCalendarSectionTitle": "Calendar & Home Widget",
  "helpCalendarThemesTitle": "Changing the calendar theme",
  "helpCalendarThemesBody": "Choose from six themes in Settings → Calendar Theme: Classic Light, Classic Dark, Simple Line, Soft Card, Diary and Dark Grid.\n\nThemes apply to the calendar inside the app. The home-screen widget keeps its light appearance.",
  "helpCalendarOtTitle": "Recording and checking overtime",
  "helpCalendarOtBody": "Tap a date in the Calendar tab to record overtime. The app includes it in your weekly and monthly work-hour totals. OT means overtime.\n\nWeekly totals always run from Monday to Sunday, even when your calendar starts on Sunday. Monthly totals use the reporting period you choose in Settings. These are time totals, not a payroll calculation.",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayTitle": "Checking weekly and monthly work hours",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayBody": "Enter the usual start and end times for each shift in Settings. ShiftBell uses these times and your overtime entries to calculate weekly and monthly hours.\n\nFor a repeating pattern, you can also include extra hours from shift changes in the overtime summary. Only the increase over the original shift counts, not the new shift’s full duration.",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetTitle": "Adding the home-screen widget",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetBody": "Long-press your home screen, open Widgets and add ShiftBell Calendar. It shows shift names and up to three notes per date. Resize it to switch between a three-week view and a full month.\n\nWeekday order follows the same regional default as the app. Public holidays and lunar dates are not shown in English.",
  "helpScheduleTabSectionTitle": "Events Tab",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItTitle": "How is the Events tab different?",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItBody": "Calendar notes apply to a whole day. Events in the Events tab have a specific time, with an optional end time and reminder. Use them for appointments, errands or plans before and after work.",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditTitle": "Adding and editing events",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditBody": "Tap the clock icon at the bottom right, then tap a time on the timeline to add an event.",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryTitle": "How matching icons get picked",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryBody": "The app reads what you typed and automatically attaches a matching icon. If it can't find a good match, a default shape icon is attached instead.",
  "helpConditionSectionTitle": "Sleep & Recovery",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItTitle": "What is the Sleep & Recovery tab?",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItBody": "This tab is available only when the app is in Korean. It combines your shift schedule and sleep records to show facts about rest between shifts, sleep after work and consecutive shifts, along with suggested actions for the current time. It does not give a health score. Missing sleep records or shift times are shown as limits on the assessment.",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListTitle": "Sleep & Recovery evidence",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListBody": "The Sleep & Recovery tab's guidance draws on the 14 sources below. A few thresholds, such as consecutive workdays or comparisons with your own average, are set by this app and don't carry a separate source.\n\nEVIDENCE-001 · Shift rotation direction — Forward rotation (day→evening→night) is reported to be easier to adapt to than backward rotation. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-002 · Minimum rest between shifts — Recommends at least 11 hours of rest between two shifts. (Knauth & Hornberger, cited in NIOSH Module 5)\n\nEVIDENCE-003 · Minimum rest between shifts (legal standard) — Every worker must get at least 11 consecutive hours of rest per 24 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 3)\n\nEVIDENCE-004 · Long shifts and injury/accident risk — Relative risk is modeled at about +13% for a 10-hour shift and +27% for a 12-hour shift, versus an 8-hour shift. (Folkard & Lombardi, 2006)\n\nEVIDENCE-005 · Rest days after consecutive shifts — Recommends 1–2 rest days after 5 days of 8-hour shifts or 4 days of 10-hour shifts, and 2 rest days after 3 days of 12-hour shifts. It also advises against packing work into a few days and then taking several days off. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-006 · Sleep after a night shift — Recommends prioritizing sleep after a night shift. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-007 · Napping before a night shift — Conditionally helpful, but the guideline itself states the evidence level is very low. Watch for grogginess right after waking. (AASM, 2025)\n\nEVIDENCE-008 · Caffeine and sleep — Caffeine taken even 6 hours before bedtime significantly cuts total sleep time by over an hour. (Drake et al., 2013)\n\nEVIDENCE-009 · Light exposure on the commute home after a night shift — Reducing bright light exposure can help later sleep (but safety comes first if you're too drowsy to drive safely). (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-010 · Eating during a night shift — Recommends eating less between midnight and 6am and favoring light meals. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-011 · Recommended sleep for adults — Adults aged 18–64 are recommended 7–9 hours of sleep. (National Sleep Foundation, 2015)\n\nEVIDENCE-012 · Maximum weekly working hours (legal standard) — Average weekly working time, including overtime, must not exceed 48 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 6)\n\nEVIDENCE-013 · Consecutive night shifts and accident risk — Relative risk of accidents/injuries accumulates with each successive night shift (about +6% on night 2, +17% on night 3, +36% on night 4 — the increase is steeper for consecutive night shifts than for consecutive day/evening shifts). (Folkard & Tucker, 2003)\n\nEVIDENCE-014 · Weekly cumulative working hours — Recommends limiting nurses' work to 12 hours in 24 and 60 hours in 7 days. A 2014 systematic review reported that working more than 40 hours a week is linked to lower patient safety. (IOM, 2004; cited in NIOSH Module 5)",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededTitle": "Setup required before using this tab",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededBody": "You need to enter start and end times for at least one shift name under Settings → Work Hours & Overtime first. Until you do, Today's Condition won't appear, but you can still log sleep. You don't need to fill in every shift — just one you're currently using is enough.",
  "helpSleepSectionTitle": "Sleep Tracking",
  "helpSleepWidgetTitle": "What is the sleep-tracking widget?",
  "helpSleepWidgetBody": "Add the sleep widget to your home screen. Tap Sleep when you go to bed and Wake when you get up. The widget also shows today’s shift.\n\nYour sleep records appear in Sleep & Recovery alongside your work schedule.",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectTitle": "Automatic sleep estimation",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectBody": "ShiftBell estimates when you may have slept using your work schedule and periods when your phone’s screen was off. It looks for sleep after work, overnight sleep and naps before a night shift. Sleep during a shift is not estimated automatically; record it using the widget.\n\nEstimates are saved as confirmed sleep records only after you review and confirm them.",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarTitle": "Reviewing and editing recent sleep records",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarBody": "The \"Recent Sleep\" mini calendar on the Sleep & Recovery tab shows confirmed records day by day. Tap a slot to create, edit, or delete a record right there. For older records, use \"View All\" to browse month by month.\n\nBelow it, \"My sleep by shift\" breaks down your average sleep for each shift type into main sleep and naps.",
  "helpFriendSectionTitle": "Friend Sharing",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItTitle": "What is friend sharing?",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItBody": "This lets you share your work schedule so a friend or someone else can view it — or view a schedule someone else shared with you. You can share your schedule easily even with people who don't have the app.",
  "helpFriendShareCodeTitle": "Generating and sharing your code",
  "helpFriendShareCodeBody": "Tap \"Friends\" on the Calendar tab, then \"Share My Schedule\" to get your code. Give it to the other person, and they can register it in their own app to view your schedule.",
  "helpFriendViewFriendTitle": "Viewing a friend's schedule with their code",
  "helpFriendViewFriendBody": "If you've received a friend's code, add them from the friend list screen by entering it — their schedule then shows up as a calendar inside your own app.",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteTitle": "What happens if you reinstall the app",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteBody": "After reinstalling ShiftBell, you will need to share a new code or link. Your old code does not connect to the new installation.\n\nSomeone using the old code may still see the schedule you shared before reinstalling, without an error message. Let them know and ask them to replace the old code with your new one.",
  "helpBackupSectionTitle": "Backup & Restore",
  "helpBackupDataStorageTitle": "Where is my data stored?",
  "helpBackupDataStorageBody": "Your shifts, alarms, alarm history, notes and work-hour records are saved on your device. Backups are local files in Download/ShiftBell. They are not uploaded automatically.\n\nUsage statistics and advertising use Google services. See the Privacy Policy for what information they process. Data from features previously used in Korean can remain in local storage and backups even while those features are hidden in English.",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupTitle": "When does automatic backup happen?",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupBody": "ShiftBell checks for changes when you open, return to or leave the app. If your data has changed, it saves an automatic backup to Download/ShiftBell on your device. You can also choose Back up data in Settings.\n\nIt keeps one manual backup and one automatic backup. A new backup replaces only the file of the same kind. Backups require Android 10 or later.",
  "helpBackupRestoreTitle": "Restoring after reinstalling or moving to a new device",
  "helpBackupRestoreBody": "Choose Restore from backup on the first screen or in Settings, then select a backup file from Download/ShiftBell. Review its saved time before restoring: it replaces your schedule, notes, overtime and other saved data and settings.\n\nAlarm history and creation records are merged with the backup. Alarms that are ringing or snoozed are kept.\n\nTo move to another phone, copy the file to that phone first. After restoring, check your shifts, upcoming alarms and alarm permissions.",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualTitle": "If automatic restore doesn't find your backup",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualBody": "After reinstalling, choose Restore from backup and select your previous backup using the file picker. Android may not let the new installation find files created by the old installation automatically.",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailTitle": "A very old backup may fail to restore",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailBody": "A backup from a much older version of ShiftBell may use a data format that can no longer be restored. Trying the same file again may not help.\n\nBefore changing phones or reinstalling, use Back up data in Settings to save a fresh backup. Copy it to a safe location if you are resetting or replacing your phone.",
  "helpTroubleshootSectionTitle": "Troubleshooting",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueTitle": "My alarm didn't go off",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueBody": "Check exact alarm permission, notification and display-over-other-apps permissions, and your phone’s battery restrictions. Then check that the date has a shift assigned, that the shift has an alarm, and that sound or vibration is enabled.",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopTitle": "I force-stopped this app from phone settings",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopBody": "Using Force stop in Android Settings → Apps cancels ShiftBell’s scheduled alarms. Open ShiftBell again before your next alarm so it can reschedule upcoming alarms. Alarms whose time has already passed will not ring retroactively.\n\nLeaving the app or removing it from Recent apps does not itself cancel alarms, although some phones apply additional background restrictions.",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueTitle": "The widget isn't updating",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueBody": "Try removing the widget and adding it again. If it still looks the same, open the app once, then go back to your home screen and check again.",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingTitle": "I can't find my backup file",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingBody": "Look in Download/ShiftBell using your phone’s file manager. If no backup is there, use Back up data in Settings to create one.",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedTitle": "My friend's schedule hasn't changed in days",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedBody": "Check the Last synced time on your friend’s schedule. An old timestamp can mean their changes have not synced or they have reinstalled ShiftBell.\n\nAsk them to open the app with an internet connection and check sharing. If they reinstalled it, ask for a new code, remove the old entry and add them again.",
  "startupFailedTitle": "Couldn't start the app",
  "startupFailedBody": "Something went wrong while getting the app ready. Make sure your device has enough free storage, then try again. If this keeps happening, try restarting your device.",
  "startupRetry": "Try again",
  "startupSlowMessage": "Getting the app ready. Please wait a moment.",
  "startupStalledMessage": "This is taking longer than usual. If you still see this screen after trying again, close the app completely and open it again.",
  "shiftAnnualLeave": "Leave",
  "onboardingBackupTitle": "Restore from backup",
  "onboardingBackupDesc": "Start from a backup file in Download/ShiftBell.",
  "helpOneTapTitle": "One-tap alarms: quickly add a saved time",
  "helpOneTapBody": "Tap the one-tap alarm button above the calendar, then + to save a time and a sound setting (sound + vibration, vibration or silent). One empty slot appears at a time, up to five saved presets.\n\nChoose a saved time, then today or tomorrow. After scheduling, the panel closes and a short confirmation appears. Past times and times already occupied by another alarm are rejected.\n\nTap the calendar while viewing the preset list to close the panel. After selecting a preset, Back returns to the list.\n\nSelecting a preset also shows Edit and Delete. A preset assigned to a future date cannot be edited until its assigned alarms are removed. To remove an individual alarm, open its date and tap × next to the one-tap alarm. Deleting a preset also deletes its assigned alarms. The confirmation explains whether a shift alarm will be restored.",
  "helpCalendarTextSizeTitle": "Calendar text and screen sizes",
  "helpCalendarTextSizeBody": "Calendar cells adapt to phone, cover and unfolded screen widths. Long shift names and notes may be shortened to fit. Tap a date or note to read the full text. Each date supports up to three notes.\n\nSimple Line and Soft Card use a larger default shift label. Calendar text scaling is limited to keep dates, shifts and notes within their cells.",
  "helpDiagnosticTitle": "Find a diagnostic file for a problem report",
  "helpDiagnosticBody": "For alarm or backup issues, check Download/ShiftBell/log in your file manager (Android 10 or later). Diagnostic files are saved and updated locally.\n\nThey include device and Android versions, permission and battery settings, and alarm and backup processing records. Note or event contents, shift names and friend names are excluded. Files are not sent automatically. You can attach them when contacting support. They may remain after uninstalling; remove them in your file manager when no longer needed.",
  "helpCalendarLocaleTitle": "Dates, weekends and public holidays",
  "helpCalendarLocaleBody": "The calendar uses Sunday first for US English and Monday first for UK English and supported European regional settings. English dates use month names to avoid confusion between day/month and month/day.\n\nPublic holidays are not included in the English calendar or widget in this release. A red Sunday is a weekend marker, not a public-holiday label. Korean lunar dates are also hidden. Your shifts and alarms still follow your own schedule.\n\nWeekly work-hour totals always use Monday–Sunday. A holiday or weekend does not automatically make a shift a day off.",
  "alarmSnoozedLabel": "Snoozed",
  "alarmClockFirstOccurrence": "First occurrence",
  "alarmClockSecondOccurrence": "Second occurrence",
  "shiftNameInUse": "Shifts in use can only be renamed.",
  "scheduleOutsideTeamsConfirm": "The selected position does not match any team in the full roster. Continuing will delete the full roster, and you will need to set it up again. Change your schedule?",
  "scheduleMyTeam": "My team",
  "scheduleTeamsAtToday": "Team positions in the cycle as of today.",
